From Mike.Prager at noaa.gov  Thu Aug  5 13:09:12 1999
From: Mike.Prager at noaa.gov (Michael Prager)
Date: Thu, 05 Aug 1999 09:09:12 -0400
Subject: [S] Watcom Compiler, Fortran, and S-Plus
In-Reply-To: <199908040630.BAA09376@wubios.wustl.edu>
References: <199908040630.BAA09376@wubios.wustl.edu>
Message-ID: <4.2.0.58.19990804104909.00a43530@mail.bea.nmfs.gov>

A few words about Fortran.  I am conscious this is not a Fortran group, so 
I will keep them brief.

Fred Balch recently wrote:

>> I found, at the Sybase site, that version 11 of Watcom's FORTRAN is 
FORTRAN 90. <<

I believe that statement to be incorrect: the formal product name is 
"Watcom FORTRAN 77 11.0".  Unless Watcom made a stealthy last-minute 
upgrade, it is FORTRAN 77 with some capabilities of Fortran 90 added, but 
not many of the most useful and powerful ones.

>> Most of the additional capabilities of FORTRAN [90/95] appear to be 
with regard to the stand-alone capability of the language (intrinsic 
arrays) and are not related to the interface. <<

For the record: Fortran 90 was a major revision, with large improvements in 
the following areas: code modularity and reusability, specification and 
control of precision, inclusion of many common extensions that facilitate 
error detection and structured programming, introduction of intrinsic array 
operations, changes to improve optimization of parallel processing, 
operator overloading, introduction of pointers, dynamic memory management 
(allocatable arrays), and better file handling.  In addition, there are 
finally standard date and time functions.  Fortran 95 was a minor revision 
of Fortran 90, mainly to further facilitate high-performance computing and 
parallel processing.  The name of the language from the 1990 standard 
onwards is "Fortran," not "FORTRAN."

Like Fred, I wonder what compiler MathSoft will use for new versions of 
S-PLUS for Windows and how MathSoft intends to maintain and improve the 
Fortran/S-PLUS interface.  I was disappointed to read (between the lines) 
that the DLL interface is troublesome, as otherwise it would seem to offer 
a compiler-independent route to integration.

